Coeliac Disease
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic steatorrhoea, Refractory iron deficiency anaemia, Dermatitis herpetiformis, Positive tTG IgA with villous atrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-012
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting severe abdominal cramping, explosive diarrhoea, and extreme physical exhaustion causing near-syncope upon standing.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage confirmed high-volume, continuous diarrhoea causing acute dehydration and symptomatic orthostasis in a patient with chronic lethargy. Urgent physical evaluation was required to assess cardiovascular stability, initiate aggressive oral rehydration, and exclude an acute surgical abdomen or severe infectious enteritis prior to the availability of a standard GP appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been in the bathroom 15 times today, my stomach is in knots, and the room spins completely when I try to stand."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden worsening of chronic episodic diarrhoea, describing the passage of bulky, foul-smelling, floating stools (steatorrhoea). Accompanied by severe recurrent abdominal bloating and painful flatulence. Reports a 6-month history of profound lethargy and unintentional weight loss despite a robust appetite. Recently diagnosed with unexplained iron deficiency anaemia refractory to oral iron supplementation. Notes intensely itchy, grouped blisters on elbows.
Functional Status: Bedbound. Attempting to ambulate to the bathroom results in severe dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, unable to safely prepare food or access hydration independently due to vertigo.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 (Supine), 85/55 (Standing) | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, fatigued, and moderately dehydrated with delayed capillary refill (3 seconds) and dry oral mucosa.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is distended, tympanic, with hyperactive borborygmi. Diffuse mild tenderness on palpation but no guarding or rigidity. Skin examination reveals excoriated grouped vesicles on the extensor surfaces of the forearms and elbows (dermatitis herpetiformis). Positive orthostatic drop in blood pressure. Presentation firmly supports chronic steatorrhoea, refractory iron deficiency anaemia, and dermatitis herpetiformis; positive tTG IgA with villous atrophy is clinically suspected pending formal serology and endoscopy.
Environmental Observations: Empty water glasses and discarded over-the-counter iron tablets on the nightstand.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking oral ferrous sulfate with no reported clinical benefit.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Coeliac Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute diarrhoeal decompensation with hypovolaemia)
Differentials:

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific presence of dermatitis herpetiformis and predominantly malabsorptive features (steatorrhoea, isolated iron deficiency) without frank hematochezia or localized right lower quadrant mass.

Giardiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prolonged, chronic timeline of the anaemia and the presence of the characteristic autoimmune blistering skin rash.

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The underlying chronic history of weight loss, refractory anaemia, and specific cutaneous findings, indicating an acute flare of a chronic malabsorptive process rather than a simple acute infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Living alone with fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute hypovolaemic crisis driven by high-volume steatorrhoea, superimposed on a chronic malabsorptive state characterized by refractory iron deficiency and an autoimmune rash. The objective finding of marked orthostatic hypotension in a patient living alone created a significant risk for syncopal falls and traumatic injury. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to safely assess volume status, provide immediate symptomatic control with anti-motility agents, and establish a structured oral rehydration protocol to prevent progression to hypovolaemic shock overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Loperamide 4mg immediately. Initiated structured oral rehydration therapy (ORT). Provided a pathology request for comprehensive Coeliac serology (tTG-IgA, total IgA, FBC, Iron studies, CMP) to be drawn urgently the following morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep a filled 1-liter water bottle on your nightstand and take three slow sips every 15 minutes while awake. This steady, measured intake prevents overwhelming your inflamed bowel, allowing it to slowly absorb the water and reverse your dangerous dizziness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Must see regular GP within 48 hours for blood test review and Gastroenterology referral.

